1
00:00:04,130 --> 00:00:05,730
INSTRUCTOR: This is a 3.

2
00:00:05,730 --> 00:00:08,420
It's sloppily written and
rendered at an extremely low

3
00:00:08,420 --> 00:00:10,980
resolution of 28-by-28 pixels.

4
00:00:10,980 --> 00:00:14,060
But your brain has no trouble
recognizing it as a three.

5
00:00:14,060 --> 00:00:16,129
And I want you to take
a moment to appreciate

6
00:00:16,129 --> 00:00:19,470
how crazy it is that brains
can do this so effortlessly.

7
00:00:19,470 --> 00:00:23,630
I mean, this, this and this are
also recognizable as 3's, even

8
00:00:23,630 --> 00:00:26,060
though the specific
values of each pixel

9
00:00:26,060 --> 00:00:28,590
is very different from
one image to the next.

10
00:00:28,590 --> 00:00:30,890
The particular light
sensitive cells

11
00:00:30,890 --> 00:00:33,860
in your eye that are firing
when you see this three

12
00:00:33,860 --> 00:00:37,230
are very different from the
ones firing when you see this 3.

13
00:00:37,230 --> 00:00:40,910
But something in that crazy,
smart visual cortex of yours

14
00:00:40,910 --> 00:00:44,000
resolves these as
representing the same idea,

15
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:46,550
while at the same time
recognizing other images

16
00:00:46,550 --> 00:00:48,920
as their own distinct ideas.

17
00:00:48,920 --> 00:00:53,120
But if I told you, hey, sit down
and write for me a program that

18
00:00:53,120 --> 00:00:57,470
takes in a grid of 28-by-28
pixels like this and outputs

19
00:00:57,470 --> 00:00:59,780
a single number
between 0 and 10,

20
00:00:59,780 --> 00:01:02,780
telling you what it
thinks the digit is, well,

21
00:01:02,780 --> 00:01:06,920
the task goes from comically
trivial to dauntingly difficult.

22
00:01:06,920 --> 00:01:08,600
Unless you've been
living under a rock,

23
00:01:08,600 --> 00:01:10,670
I think I hardly need to
motivate the relevance

24
00:01:10,670 --> 00:01:13,160
and importance of machine
learning and neural networks

25
00:01:13,160 --> 00:01:14,970
to the present
and to the future.

26
00:01:14,970 --> 00:01:16,460
But what I want
to do here is show

27
00:01:16,460 --> 00:01:18,200
you what a neural
network actually

28
00:01:18,200 --> 00:01:20,630
is, assuming no
background, and to help

29
00:01:20,630 --> 00:01:23,150
visualize what it's
doing, not as a buzzword,

30
00:01:23,150 --> 00:01:24,750
but as a piece of math.

31
00:01:24,750 --> 00:01:27,650
My hope is just that you come
away feeling like the structure

32
00:01:27,650 --> 00:01:31,370
itself is motivated and to feel
what it means when you read

33
00:01:31,370 --> 00:01:33,860
or you hear about a neural
network, quote unquote,

34
00:01:33,860 --> 00:01:35,060
"learning."

35
00:01:35,060 --> 00:01:37,533
This video is just going to
be devoted to the structure

36
00:01:37,533 --> 00:01:39,200
component of that,
and the following one

37
00:01:39,200 --> 00:01:40,620
is going to tackle learning.

38
00:01:40,620 --> 00:01:42,290
What we're going to
do is put together

39
00:01:42,290 --> 00:01:45,985
a neural network that can learn
to recognize handwritten digits.

40
00:01:49,410 --> 00:01:51,300
This is a somewhat
classic example

41
00:01:51,300 --> 00:01:52,520
for introducing the topic.

42
00:01:52,520 --> 00:01:54,270
And I'm happy to stick
with the status quo

43
00:01:54,270 --> 00:01:56,163
here because at the
end of the two videos,

44
00:01:56,163 --> 00:01:58,080
I want to point you to
a couple good resources

45
00:01:58,080 --> 00:02:00,450
where you can learn more
and where you can download

46
00:02:00,450 --> 00:02:04,830
the code that does this and play
with it on your own computer.

47
00:02:04,830 --> 00:02:07,950
There are many, many
variants of neural networks.

48
00:02:07,950 --> 00:02:10,949
And in recent years, there's
been sort of a boom in research

49
00:02:10,949 --> 00:02:12,370
towards these variants.

50
00:02:12,370 --> 00:02:14,640
But in these two
introductory videos,

51
00:02:14,640 --> 00:02:17,520
you and I are just going to look
at the simplest plain vanilla

52
00:02:17,520 --> 00:02:19,510
form with no added frills.

53
00:02:19,510 --> 00:02:21,600
This is kind of a
necessary prerequisite

54
00:02:21,600 --> 00:02:24,640
for understanding any of the
more powerful modern variants.

55
00:02:24,640 --> 00:02:27,450
And trust me, it still has
plenty of complexity for us

56
00:02:27,450 --> 00:02:28,890
to wrap our minds around.

57
00:02:28,890 --> 00:02:30,930
But even in this
simplest form, it

58
00:02:30,930 --> 00:02:33,330
can learn to recognize
handwritten digits, which

59
00:02:33,330 --> 00:02:37,230
is a pretty cool thing for
a computer to be able to do.

60
00:02:37,230 --> 00:02:39,060
And at the same
time, you'll see how

61
00:02:39,060 --> 00:02:43,450
it does fall short of a couple
hopes that we might have for it.

62
00:02:43,450 --> 00:02:45,430
As the name suggests,
neural networks

63
00:02:45,430 --> 00:02:47,210
are inspired by the brain.

64
00:02:47,210 --> 00:02:48,740
But let's break that down.

65
00:02:48,740 --> 00:02:49,790
What are the neurons?

66
00:02:49,790 --> 00:02:52,210
And in what sense are
they linked together?

67
00:02:52,210 --> 00:02:55,930
Now, when I say neuron, all
I want you to think about

68
00:02:55,930 --> 00:02:59,320
is a thing that holds a
number, specifically a number

69
00:02:59,320 --> 00:03:00,690
between 0 and 1.

70
00:03:00,690 --> 00:03:03,550
It's really not more than that.

71
00:03:03,550 --> 00:03:06,190
For example, the network
starts with a bunch

72
00:03:06,190 --> 00:03:09,010
of neurons corresponding
to each of the 28 times

73
00:03:09,010 --> 00:03:14,490
28 pixels of the input image,
which is 784 neurons in total.

74
00:03:14,490 --> 00:03:16,660
Each one of these
holds a number that

75
00:03:16,660 --> 00:03:20,680
represents the grayscale value
of the corresponding pixel,

76
00:03:20,680 --> 00:03:25,000
ranging from 0 for black pixels
up to 1 for white pixels.

77
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:28,750
This number inside the neuron
is called its activation.

78
00:03:28,750 --> 00:03:30,250
And the image you
might have in mind

79
00:03:30,250 --> 00:03:33,340
here is that each neuron is
lit up when its activation is

80
00:03:33,340 --> 00:03:36,390
a high number.

81
00:03:36,390 --> 00:03:39,630
So all of these
784 neurons make up

82
00:03:39,630 --> 00:03:41,615
the first layer of our network.

83
00:03:46,200 --> 00:03:48,130
Now jumping over
to the last layer,

84
00:03:48,130 --> 00:03:51,720
this has 10 neurons, each
representing one of the digits.

85
00:03:51,720 --> 00:03:55,080
The activation in these
neurons, again, some number

86
00:03:55,080 --> 00:03:58,260
that's between 0 and
1 represents how much

87
00:03:58,260 --> 00:04:01,740
the system thinks that a given
image corresponds with a given

88
00:04:01,740 --> 00:04:02,580
digit.

89
00:04:02,580 --> 00:04:05,220
There's also a couple
layers in between called

90
00:04:05,220 --> 00:04:07,950
the hidden layers, which,
for the time being,

91
00:04:07,950 --> 00:04:10,470
should just be a giant
question mark for how

92
00:04:10,470 --> 00:04:12,660
on Earth this process
of recognizing digits

93
00:04:12,660 --> 00:04:13,950
is going to be handled.

94
00:04:13,950 --> 00:04:16,260
In this network, I
chose two hidden layers,

95
00:04:16,260 --> 00:04:17,970
each one with 16 neurons.

96
00:04:17,970 --> 00:04:20,860
And admittedly, that's kind
of an arbitrary choice.

97
00:04:20,860 --> 00:04:22,560
To be honest, I chose
two layers based

98
00:04:22,560 --> 00:04:25,150
on how I want to motivate the
structure in just a moment.

99
00:04:25,150 --> 00:04:27,390
And 16, well, that
was just a nice number

100
00:04:27,390 --> 00:04:28,570
to fit on the screen.

101
00:04:28,570 --> 00:04:30,150
In practice, there
is a lot of room

102
00:04:30,150 --> 00:04:32,880
for experiment with the
specific structure here.

103
00:04:32,880 --> 00:04:35,940
The way the network operates,
activations in one layer

104
00:04:35,940 --> 00:04:38,910
determine the activations
of the next layer.

105
00:04:38,910 --> 00:04:41,760
And of course, the heart of
the network as an information

106
00:04:41,760 --> 00:04:45,180
processing mechanism
comes down to exactly how

107
00:04:45,180 --> 00:04:47,920
those activations from one
layer bring about activations

108
00:04:47,920 --> 00:04:49,120
in the next layer.

109
00:04:49,120 --> 00:04:51,730
It's meant to be
loosely analogous to how

110
00:04:51,730 --> 00:04:53,650
in biological
networks of neurons,

111
00:04:53,650 --> 00:04:57,850
some groups of neurons firing
cause certain others to fire.

112
00:04:57,850 --> 00:04:59,950
Now the network I'm
showing here has already

113
00:04:59,950 --> 00:05:01,790
been trained to
recognize digits.

114
00:05:01,790 --> 00:05:03,650
And let me show you
what I mean by that.

115
00:05:03,650 --> 00:05:06,430
It means if you feed
in an image lighting up

116
00:05:06,430 --> 00:05:09,640
all 784 neurons of
the input layer,

117
00:05:09,640 --> 00:05:12,520
according to the brightness
of each pixel in the image,

118
00:05:12,520 --> 00:05:15,130
that pattern of
activations causes

119
00:05:15,130 --> 00:05:17,890
some very specific pattern
in the next layer, which

120
00:05:17,890 --> 00:05:20,710
causes some pattern in the one
after it, which finally gives

121
00:05:20,710 --> 00:05:22,550
some pattern in
the output layer.

122
00:05:22,550 --> 00:05:25,510
And the brightest neuron
of that output layer

123
00:05:25,510 --> 00:05:28,120
is the network's choice,
so to speak, for what

124
00:05:28,120 --> 00:05:29,576
digit this image represents.

125
00:05:32,350 --> 00:05:35,410
And before jumping into the math
for how one layer influences

126
00:05:35,410 --> 00:05:37,630
the next or how
training works, let's

127
00:05:37,630 --> 00:05:39,760
just talk about why
it's even reasonable

128
00:05:39,760 --> 00:05:42,010
to expect a layered
structure like this

129
00:05:42,010 --> 00:05:43,940
to behave intelligently.

130
00:05:43,940 --> 00:05:45,360
What are we expecting here?

131
00:05:45,360 --> 00:05:47,830
What is the best hope for
what those middle layers might

132
00:05:47,830 --> 00:05:48,970
be doing?

133
00:05:48,970 --> 00:05:51,700
Well, when you or
I recognize digits,

134
00:05:51,700 --> 00:05:54,050
we piece together
various components.

135
00:05:54,050 --> 00:05:57,080
A 9 has a loop up top
and a line on the right.

136
00:05:57,080 --> 00:05:59,890
An 8 also has a loop
up top, but it's paired

137
00:05:59,890 --> 00:06:01,930
with another loop down low.

138
00:06:01,930 --> 00:06:06,220
A 4 basically breaks down into
three specific lines and things

139
00:06:06,220 --> 00:06:07,360
like that.

140
00:06:07,360 --> 00:06:09,370
Now, in a perfect
world, we might

141
00:06:09,370 --> 00:06:12,310
hope that each neuron in
the second to last layer

142
00:06:12,310 --> 00:06:16,060
corresponds with one of these
subcomponents, that any time you

143
00:06:16,060 --> 00:06:20,050
feed in an image with, say, a
loop up top like a 9 or an 8,

144
00:06:20,050 --> 00:06:22,630
there's some specific
neuron whose activation

145
00:06:22,630 --> 00:06:24,170
is going to be close to 1.

146
00:06:24,170 --> 00:06:26,730
And I don't mean this
specific loop of pixels.

147
00:06:26,730 --> 00:06:29,440
The hope would be that any
generally loopy pattern

148
00:06:29,440 --> 00:06:32,210
towards the top sets
off this neuron.

149
00:06:32,210 --> 00:06:35,560
That way, going from the third
layer to the last one just

150
00:06:35,560 --> 00:06:38,620
requires learning which
combination of subcomponents

151
00:06:38,620 --> 00:06:40,750
corresponds to which digits.

152
00:06:40,750 --> 00:06:43,090
Of course, that just kicks
the problem down the road

153
00:06:43,090 --> 00:06:45,400
because how would you
recognize these subcomponents

154
00:06:45,400 --> 00:06:47,652
or even learn what the right
subcomponents should be.

155
00:06:47,652 --> 00:06:49,360
And I still haven't
even talked about how

156
00:06:49,360 --> 00:06:51,110
one layer influences the next.

157
00:06:51,110 --> 00:06:53,360
But run with me on
this one for a moment.

158
00:06:53,360 --> 00:06:57,030
Recognizing a loop can also
break down into subproblems.

159
00:06:57,030 --> 00:06:58,990
One reasonable way
to do this would

160
00:06:58,990 --> 00:07:02,020
be to first recognize the
various little edges that

161
00:07:02,020 --> 00:07:03,310
make it up.

162
00:07:03,310 --> 00:07:05,830
Similarly, a long
line, like the kind

163
00:07:05,830 --> 00:07:08,860
you might see in the
digits 1 or 4 or 7, well,

164
00:07:08,860 --> 00:07:11,230
that's really just a
long edge, or maybe you

165
00:07:11,230 --> 00:07:14,770
think of it as a certain pattern
of several smaller edges.

166
00:07:14,770 --> 00:07:19,120
So maybe our hope is that each
neuron in the second layer

167
00:07:19,120 --> 00:07:22,330
of the network corresponds with
the various relevant little

168
00:07:22,330 --> 00:07:23,260
edges.

169
00:07:23,260 --> 00:07:26,320
Maybe when an image
like this one comes in,

170
00:07:26,320 --> 00:07:29,170
it lights up all of
the neurons associated

171
00:07:29,170 --> 00:07:32,540
with around 8 to 10
specific little edges, which

172
00:07:32,540 --> 00:07:34,580
in turn lights up the
neurons associated

173
00:07:34,580 --> 00:07:37,320
with the upper loop and
a long vertical line,

174
00:07:37,320 --> 00:07:40,400
and those light up the
neuron associated with a 9.

175
00:07:40,400 --> 00:07:43,190
Whether or not this is what
our final network actually does

176
00:07:43,190 --> 00:07:45,890
is another question, one
that I'll come back to once

177
00:07:45,890 --> 00:07:47,560
we see how to train the network.

178
00:07:47,560 --> 00:07:49,730
But this is a hope
that we might have,

179
00:07:49,730 --> 00:07:52,940
a sort of goal with the
layered structure like this.

180
00:07:52,940 --> 00:07:54,860
Moreover, you can
imagine how being

181
00:07:54,860 --> 00:07:57,200
able to detect edges
and patterns like this

182
00:07:57,200 --> 00:08:00,780
would be really useful for
other image recognition tasks.

183
00:08:00,780 --> 00:08:02,512
And even beyond
image recognition,

184
00:08:02,512 --> 00:08:04,220
there are all sorts
of intelligent things

185
00:08:04,220 --> 00:08:07,770
you might want to do that break
down into layers of abstraction.

186
00:08:07,770 --> 00:08:11,180
Parsing speech, for example,
involves taking raw audio

187
00:08:11,180 --> 00:08:13,400
and picking out
distinct sounds, which

188
00:08:13,400 --> 00:08:16,160
combine to make certain
syllables, which combine

189
00:08:16,160 --> 00:08:18,890
to form words, which combine
to make up phrases and more

190
00:08:18,890 --> 00:08:20,930
abstract thoughts, et cetera.

191
00:08:20,930 --> 00:08:23,840
But getting back to how
any of this actually works,

192
00:08:23,840 --> 00:08:25,940
picture yourself
right now designing

193
00:08:25,940 --> 00:08:28,310
how exactly the
activations in one layer

194
00:08:28,310 --> 00:08:30,930
might determine the
activations in the next.

195
00:08:30,930 --> 00:08:34,549
The goal is to have some
mechanism that could conceivably

196
00:08:34,549 --> 00:08:38,450
combine pixels into edges or
edges into patterns or patterns

197
00:08:38,450 --> 00:08:39,360
into digits.

198
00:08:39,360 --> 00:08:42,140
And to zoom in on one
very specific example,

199
00:08:42,140 --> 00:08:45,050
let's say the hope is
for one particular neuron

200
00:08:45,050 --> 00:08:48,530
in the second layer to pick up
on whether or not the image has

201
00:08:48,530 --> 00:08:51,200
an edge in this region here.

202
00:08:51,200 --> 00:08:54,050
The question at hand
is, what parameters

203
00:08:54,050 --> 00:08:55,500
should the network have?

204
00:08:55,500 --> 00:08:57,680
What dials and
knobs should you be

205
00:08:57,680 --> 00:09:00,230
able to tweak so that
it's expressive enough

206
00:09:00,230 --> 00:09:02,420
to potentially
capture this pattern

207
00:09:02,420 --> 00:09:05,960
or any other pixel pattern or
the pattern that several edges

208
00:09:05,960 --> 00:09:08,480
can make, a loop and
other such things?

209
00:09:08,480 --> 00:09:10,880
Well, what we'll do
is assign a weight

210
00:09:10,880 --> 00:09:13,920
to each one of the
connections between our neuron

211
00:09:13,920 --> 00:09:16,070
and the neurons from
the first layer.

212
00:09:16,070 --> 00:09:18,380
These weights are just numbers.

213
00:09:18,380 --> 00:09:22,400
Then take all of those
activations from the first layer

214
00:09:22,400 --> 00:09:27,308
and compute their weighted sum,
according to these weights.

215
00:09:27,308 --> 00:09:29,100
I find it helpful to
think of these weights

216
00:09:29,100 --> 00:09:32,130
as being organized into a
little grid of their own.

217
00:09:32,130 --> 00:09:33,720
And I'm going to
use green pixels

218
00:09:33,720 --> 00:09:35,970
to indicate positive
weights and red pixels

219
00:09:35,970 --> 00:09:37,740
to indicate negative
weights, where

220
00:09:37,740 --> 00:09:40,770
the brightness of that pixel
is some loose depiction

221
00:09:40,770 --> 00:09:42,510
of the weights value.

222
00:09:42,510 --> 00:09:45,000
Now, if we made the weights
associated with almost all

223
00:09:45,000 --> 00:09:48,570
of the pixels 0 except for some
positive weights in this region

224
00:09:48,570 --> 00:09:51,870
that we care about, then
taking the weighted sum of all

225
00:09:51,870 --> 00:09:53,790
the pixel values
really just amounts

226
00:09:53,790 --> 00:09:55,740
to adding up the
values of the pixel

227
00:09:55,740 --> 00:09:58,810
just in the region
that we care about.

228
00:09:58,810 --> 00:10:00,430
And if you really
wanted to pick up

229
00:10:00,430 --> 00:10:03,220
on whether there's an edge
here, what you might do

230
00:10:03,220 --> 00:10:06,160
is have some negative weights
associated with the surrounding

231
00:10:06,160 --> 00:10:07,090
pixels.

232
00:10:07,090 --> 00:10:10,660
Then the sum is largest when
those middle pixels are bright,

233
00:10:10,660 --> 00:10:14,450
but the surrounding
pixels are darker.

234
00:10:14,450 --> 00:10:16,460
When you compute a
weighted sum like this,

235
00:10:16,460 --> 00:10:18,510
you might come out
with any number.

236
00:10:18,510 --> 00:10:20,240
But for this
network, what we want

237
00:10:20,240 --> 00:10:23,920
is for activations to be
some value between 0 and 1.

238
00:10:23,920 --> 00:10:26,870
So a common thing to do is
to pump this weighted sum

239
00:10:26,870 --> 00:10:29,840
into some function that
squishes the real number line

240
00:10:29,840 --> 00:10:32,270
into the range between 0 and 1.

241
00:10:32,270 --> 00:10:33,860
And a common function
that does this

242
00:10:33,860 --> 00:10:37,760
is called the sigmoid function,
also known as a logistic curve.

243
00:10:37,760 --> 00:10:41,250
Basically, very negative
inputs end up close to 0,

244
00:10:41,250 --> 00:10:43,700
very positive inputs
end up close to 1,

245
00:10:43,700 --> 00:10:46,540
and it just steadily
increases around the input 0.

246
00:10:49,200 --> 00:10:51,600
So the activation
of the neuron here

247
00:10:51,600 --> 00:10:55,500
is basically a measure of how
positive the relevant weighted

248
00:10:55,500 --> 00:10:57,540
sum is.

249
00:10:57,540 --> 00:11:00,270
But maybe it's not that you
want the neuron to light up

250
00:11:00,270 --> 00:11:02,230
when the weighted
sum is bigger than 0.

251
00:11:02,230 --> 00:11:05,160
Maybe you only want it to be
active when the sum is bigger

252
00:11:05,160 --> 00:11:06,750
than, say, 10.

253
00:11:06,750 --> 00:11:10,980
That is, you want some
bias for it to be inactive.

254
00:11:10,980 --> 00:11:13,890
What we'll do then is just
add in some other number

255
00:11:13,890 --> 00:11:16,380
like negative 10 to
this weighted sum

256
00:11:16,380 --> 00:11:19,230
before plugging it through
the sigmoid squishification

257
00:11:19,230 --> 00:11:20,340
function.

258
00:11:20,340 --> 00:11:23,100
That additional number
is called the bias.

259
00:11:23,100 --> 00:11:26,580
So the weights tell you what
pixel pattern this neuron

260
00:11:26,580 --> 00:11:28,440
in the second layer
is picking up on.

261
00:11:28,440 --> 00:11:31,440
And the bias tells you
how high the weighted sum

262
00:11:31,440 --> 00:11:33,750
needs to be before
the neuron starts

263
00:11:33,750 --> 00:11:35,880
getting meaningfully active.

264
00:11:35,880 --> 00:11:38,020
And that is just one neuron.

265
00:11:38,020 --> 00:11:40,800
Every other neuron
in this layer is

266
00:11:40,800 --> 00:11:44,010
going to be connected
to all 784 pixel neurons

267
00:11:44,010 --> 00:11:45,370
from the first layer.

268
00:11:45,370 --> 00:11:48,430
And each one of
those 784 connections

269
00:11:48,430 --> 00:11:51,460
has its own weight
associated with it.

270
00:11:51,460 --> 00:11:54,460
Also, each one has some
bias, some other number

271
00:11:54,460 --> 00:11:55,960
that you add on to
the weighted sum

272
00:11:55,960 --> 00:11:58,090
before squishing it
with the sigmoid.

273
00:11:58,090 --> 00:11:59,830
And that's a lot to think about.

274
00:11:59,830 --> 00:12:02,420
With this hidden
layer of 16 neurons,

275
00:12:02,420 --> 00:12:08,720
that's a total of 784 times 16
weights along with 16 biases.

276
00:12:08,720 --> 00:12:11,440
And all of that is just the
connections from the first layer

277
00:12:11,440 --> 00:12:12,410
to the second.

278
00:12:12,410 --> 00:12:14,350
The connections between
the other layers

279
00:12:14,350 --> 00:12:18,130
also have a bunch of weights
and biases associated with them.

280
00:12:18,130 --> 00:12:22,960
All said and done, this network
has almost exactly 13,000 total

281
00:12:22,960 --> 00:12:27,190
weights and biases, 13,000 knobs
and dials that can be tweaked

282
00:12:27,190 --> 00:12:30,730
and turned to make this network
behave in different ways.

283
00:12:30,730 --> 00:12:33,400
So when we talk about
learning, what that's

284
00:12:33,400 --> 00:12:35,470
referring to is
getting the computer

285
00:12:35,470 --> 00:12:39,070
to find a valid setting for all
of these many, many numbers,

286
00:12:39,070 --> 00:12:42,340
so that it'll actually
solve the problem at hand.

287
00:12:42,340 --> 00:12:44,860
One thought experiment
that is at once fun

288
00:12:44,860 --> 00:12:48,070
and kind of horrifying is to
imagine sitting down and setting

289
00:12:48,070 --> 00:12:50,170
all of these weights
and biases by hand,

290
00:12:50,170 --> 00:12:52,990
purposefully tweaking the
numbers so that the second layer

291
00:12:52,990 --> 00:12:54,970
picks up on edges,
the third layer

292
00:12:54,970 --> 00:12:57,130
picks up on patterns, et cetera.

293
00:12:57,130 --> 00:12:59,440
I personally find this
satisfying rather than

294
00:12:59,440 --> 00:13:01,910
just treating the network
as a total black box,

295
00:13:01,910 --> 00:13:05,350
because when the network doesn't
perform the way you anticipate,

296
00:13:05,350 --> 00:13:07,960
if you've built up a little
bit of a relationship with what

297
00:13:07,960 --> 00:13:10,030
those weights and
biases actually mean,

298
00:13:10,030 --> 00:13:13,000
you have a starting place for
experimenting with how to change

299
00:13:13,000 --> 00:13:14,620
the structure to improve.

300
00:13:14,620 --> 00:13:16,840
Or when the network
does work, but not

301
00:13:16,840 --> 00:13:18,350
for the reasons
you might expect,

302
00:13:18,350 --> 00:13:21,048
digging into what the
weights and biases are doing

303
00:13:21,048 --> 00:13:22,840
is a good way to
challenge your assumptions

304
00:13:22,840 --> 00:13:26,440
and really expose the full
space of possible solutions.

305
00:13:26,440 --> 00:13:29,500
By the way, the actual function
here is a little cumbersome

306
00:13:29,500 --> 00:13:32,500
to write down, don't you think?

307
00:13:32,500 --> 00:13:35,620
So let me show you a more
notationally compact way

308
00:13:35,620 --> 00:13:37,303
that these connections
are represented.

309
00:13:37,303 --> 00:13:39,220
This is how you'd see
it if you choose to read

310
00:13:39,220 --> 00:13:40,990
up more about neural networks.

311
00:13:40,990 --> 00:13:43,960
Organize all of the
activations from one layer

312
00:13:43,960 --> 00:13:47,560
into a column as a vector.

313
00:13:47,560 --> 00:13:50,530
Then organize all of
the weights as a matrix,

314
00:13:50,530 --> 00:13:53,380
where each row of that
matrix corresponds

315
00:13:53,380 --> 00:13:55,270
to the connections
between one layer

316
00:13:55,270 --> 00:13:58,150
and a particular neuron
in the next layer.

317
00:13:58,150 --> 00:14:00,850
What that means is that
taking the weighted sum

318
00:14:00,850 --> 00:14:02,680
of the activations
in the first layer,

319
00:14:02,680 --> 00:14:04,510
according to these
weights, corresponds

320
00:14:04,510 --> 00:14:06,970
to one of the terms
in the matrix vector

321
00:14:06,970 --> 00:14:09,390
product of everything we
have on the left here.

322
00:14:13,670 --> 00:14:15,950
By the way, so much of
machine learning just

323
00:14:15,950 --> 00:14:18,580
comes down to having a good
grasp of linear algebra.

324
00:14:18,580 --> 00:14:21,110
So for any of you who want
a nice visual understanding

325
00:14:21,110 --> 00:14:24,260
for matrices and what matrix
vector multiplication means,

326
00:14:24,260 --> 00:14:27,740
take a look at the series I did
on linear algebra, especially

327
00:14:27,740 --> 00:14:28,940
chapter 3.

328
00:14:28,940 --> 00:14:31,160
Back to our expression,
instead of talking

329
00:14:31,160 --> 00:14:33,620
about adding the bias to
each one of these values

330
00:14:33,620 --> 00:14:37,610
independently, we represent it
by organizing all those biases

331
00:14:37,610 --> 00:14:40,250
into a vector and
adding the entire vector

332
00:14:40,250 --> 00:14:42,980
to the previous
matrix vector product.

333
00:14:42,980 --> 00:14:47,000
Then as a final step, I'll wrap
a sigmoid around the outside

334
00:14:47,000 --> 00:14:47,510
here.

335
00:14:47,510 --> 00:14:49,267
And what that's
supposed to represent

336
00:14:49,267 --> 00:14:51,350
is that you're going to
apply the sigmoid function

337
00:14:51,350 --> 00:14:55,590
to each specific component of
the resulting vector inside.

338
00:14:55,590 --> 00:14:59,070
So once you write down this
weight matrix and these vectors

339
00:14:59,070 --> 00:15:02,580
as their own symbols, you can
communicate the full transition

340
00:15:02,580 --> 00:15:04,920
of activations from
one layer to the next

341
00:15:04,920 --> 00:15:07,950
in an extremely tight and
neat little expression.

342
00:15:07,950 --> 00:15:11,040
And this makes the relevant
code both a lot simpler

343
00:15:11,040 --> 00:15:14,490
and a lot faster since many
libraries optimize the heck out

344
00:15:14,490 --> 00:15:17,480
of matrix multiplication.

345
00:15:17,480 --> 00:15:19,670
Remember how earlier
I said, these neurons

346
00:15:19,670 --> 00:15:21,890
are simply things
that hold numbers?

347
00:15:21,890 --> 00:15:24,770
Well, of course, the specific
numbers that they hold

348
00:15:24,770 --> 00:15:27,920
depends on the
image you feed in.

349
00:15:27,920 --> 00:15:30,590
So it's actually more accurate
to think of each neuron

350
00:15:30,590 --> 00:15:34,400
as a function, one that
takes in the outputs of all

351
00:15:34,400 --> 00:15:36,080
the neurons in
the previous layer

352
00:15:36,080 --> 00:15:38,900
and spits out a number
between 0 and 1.

353
00:15:38,900 --> 00:15:40,760
Really, the entire
network is just

354
00:15:40,760 --> 00:15:44,750
a function, one that takes
in 784 numbers as an input

355
00:15:44,750 --> 00:15:47,570
and spits out 10
numbers as an output.

356
00:15:47,570 --> 00:15:49,670
It's an absurdly
complicated function,

357
00:15:49,670 --> 00:15:52,850
one that involves 13,000
parameters in the forms of these

358
00:15:52,850 --> 00:15:55,910
weights and biases that pick up
on certain patterns and which

359
00:15:55,910 --> 00:15:59,060
involves iterating many matrix
vector products and the sigmoid

360
00:15:59,060 --> 00:16:00,680
squishification function.

361
00:16:00,680 --> 00:16:03,380
But it's just a
function nonetheless.

362
00:16:03,380 --> 00:16:05,540
And in a way, it's
kind of reassuring

363
00:16:05,540 --> 00:16:07,100
that it looks complicated.

364
00:16:07,100 --> 00:16:09,203
I mean, if it were
any simpler, what hope

365
00:16:09,203 --> 00:16:11,870
would we have that it could take
on the challenge of recognizing

366
00:16:11,870 --> 00:16:13,010
digits?

367
00:16:13,010 --> 00:16:14,970
And how does it take
on that challenge?

368
00:16:14,970 --> 00:16:18,270
How does this network learn the
appropriate weights and biases

369
00:16:18,270 --> 00:16:20,160
just by looking at data?

370
00:16:20,160 --> 00:16:22,180
Well, that's what I'll
show in the next video.

371
00:16:22,180 --> 00:16:23,910
And I'll also dig a
little more into what

372
00:16:23,910 --> 00:16:27,310
this particular network
we're seeing is really doing.

373
00:16:27,310 --> 00:16:29,560
Now is the point I suppose
I should say subscribe

374
00:16:29,560 --> 00:16:32,320
to stay notified about when
that video or any new videos

375
00:16:32,320 --> 00:16:33,020
come out.

376
00:16:33,020 --> 00:16:35,290
But realistically, most
of you don't actually

377
00:16:35,290 --> 00:16:37,810
receive notifications
from YouTube, do you?

378
00:16:37,810 --> 00:16:39,970
Maybe more, honestly,
I should say subscribe,

379
00:16:39,970 --> 00:16:42,310
so that the neural networks
that underlie YouTube's

380
00:16:42,310 --> 00:16:44,320
recommendation
algorithm are primed

381
00:16:44,320 --> 00:16:46,750
to believe that you want to
see content from this channel

382
00:16:46,750 --> 00:16:48,190
get recommended to you.

383
00:16:48,190 --> 00:16:50,440
Anyway, stay posted for more.

384
00:16:50,440 --> 00:16:52,960
Thank you very much to everyone
supporting these videos

385
00:16:52,960 --> 00:16:53,842
on Patreon.

386
00:16:53,842 --> 00:16:55,300
I've been a little
